## Goal
Produce a two dimensional PCA of the RNA-seq samples from the gene counts, with points coloured by condition. This is a descriptive view of sample relationships, not a differential expression test.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
1. Submit one Python script through the configured batch executor, using the `gars-bio` environment and its installed pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn, and matplotlib. Read the resolved `counts_gene` TSV and `design` CSV without modifying either input.
2. Use the gene count table’s gene identifier column as the index; align its sample columns to `sample_id` in the design table and fail clearly if sample IDs do not match exactly.
3. Transform counts as `log2(count + 1)`. Select the 500 genes with highest variance across samples (or all genes if fewer than 500), then fit scikit-learn `PCA(n_components=2)` to samples by genes.
4. Plot PC1 against PC2, colour points by the `condition` column, label each point with its sample ID, include explained variance percentages on axis labels and a condition legend, and save the figure as a 200 dpi PNG. No inferential statistics are computed.

